<!--
  This is an EXAMPLE topic file. A topics/<noun>.md file answers one question:
  "when this noun comes up, where does the authoritative answer live, and what
  should I NOT treat as the source?" Copy this file to topics/<your-noun>.md
  (name it after the noun the user actually says), then replace the body.

  Good candidates for a topic file: a service whose URL/config has one true
  home, an internal tool, a project codename, an account or environment ID —
  anything where you keep re-deriving a fact that has a single canonical source.

  Keep it short: a topic file is a POINTER to the source of truth, not a copy of
  it. Copying invites drift; pointing stays correct.

  Delete this comment and the placeholder below once you write a real one. An
  empty stub is better than placeholder text — Claude treats body text as fact.
-->

# Example — canonical source for `<noun>`

The truth for `<noun>` lives in `<exact location>` (e.g. a specific repo path, a
config file, a runbook, or a ticket). Read it there; do not reconstruct the
answer from a derived or cached view, and do not guess.

- Canonical source: `<where the authoritative value is defined>`
- Derived views (do NOT treat as truth): `<dashboards, caches, exported copies>`
- Why this matters: `<one line — e.g. "the derived copy lags and has burned us">`
